From 0b8f865fd466188a7eda0328cec61e213a06ce22 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 08:16:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/15] dos: Shed onion service descriptor fetches over a byte
 budget

Nothing bounds the descriptor bytes an HSDir answers per second. A
flood of fetches such as the one in tor#41339 takes as much as the
relay can serve, and every descriptor's clients compete with it.

Add HSDirFetchBudget, the descriptor bytes per second an HSDir answers
before it sheds fetches. If it is 0, the default, it is
HSDirFetchBudgetPercent (a consensus parameter, default 50) percent of
the lower of the relay's configured and observed bandwidth, and at
least 512 KB. No fetch is refused until a second goes over the budget.
In the second after one over the budget, fetches still within their
descriptor's HSDirDescriptorFetchRate are answered first, up to half the
budget, and the others by a lottery whose odds would have kept the
previous second within it, so a flood of a few descriptors is shed
before the fetches of the others. No second answers more than one and a
half times the budget, unless a single descriptor is larger than that;
this also bounds a flood that pauses every other second. The new
includes for the bandwidth estimates raise dos.c's practracker
dependency-violation exception to 8.

A shed fetch closes its circuit with END_CIRC_REASON_RESOURCELIMIT, as
HSDirOneRequestPerCircuit does, and is counted as action "refused" in
tor_relay_hsdir_get_total. Clients retry it at another of the
descriptor's HSDirs; a 503 would also make them avoid this relay for
other directory requests. Shedding is enforced only on public relays
with HSDirDescriptorFetchLimit, an AUTOBOOL over the consensus
parameter of the same name (default 0). Either way, fetches while over
the budget, and those that are or would be shed, are counted as
tor_relay_dos_total{type="hsdir_fetch_pressure"} and
{type="hsdir_fetch_shed"}, so an operator can check a budget first.

Unlike refusing every fetch over a per-descriptor rate, this refuses
nothing until the relay goes over its budget, so denying a descriptor
at one HSDir takes about as many fetches as loading the relay. While it
sheds, whether a fetch is answered first still depends on the
descriptor's shared rate limiter, so someone who keeps the HSDir over
its budget can learn roughly how often a descriptor is fetched there. A
flood spread over many descriptors, each within its rate, is shed by
the lottery like any other, at the budget.

Part of #41339.
